\title{Consciousness as an Emergent Fixed Point:\\Diagonal Self-Models, Strange Loops, and Observational Limits}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 30, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study an extensional model of self-representation. A state space $A$ and observation space $B$ are linked by an interpretation map $I:A\to(A\to B)$, so each state represents a $B$-valued observer of the entire state space. The diagonal observation is $d(a)=I(a)(a)$. We call the model complete when every function $A\to B$ is represented. A diagonal argument shows that, for every endomorphism $g:B\to B$, completeness produces a state $a$ representing $x\mapsto g(d(x))$ and satisfying $g(d(a))=d(a)$. This witness is a self-loop in the orbit graph of $g$ and determines closed walks of every finite length.

Conversely, any fixed-point-free endomorphism of $B$ rules out complete self-modeling. Boolean negation gives an immediate impossibility theorem. Every set with two distinct elements admits a fixed-point-free endomorphism, so completeness forces $B$ to have at most one element. Together with necessary inhabitation conditions, this yields an exact classification: a complete self-model exists precisely when $A$ and $B$ are nonempty and $B$ is a subsingleton. Finally, a concrete Yoneda argument shows that a map is recovered from its precomposition action on all probes. The results clarify both the power and the limitation of unrestricted self-modeling: diagonal closure forces fixed points, but nontrivial observational distinction prevents completeness.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Self-reference occurs whenever a representational system can turn its descriptive resources upon itself. In logic, diagonal constructions produce self-referential sentences; in computation, recursion principles produce programs related to descriptions of their own behavior; in cognitive discourse, strange loops describe systems that model themselves modeling themselves. We isolate a minimal set-theoretic core of this pattern.

A system has possible states $A$ and observations $B$. At each state it carries an observer of the entire state space, giving a map
\[
I:A\longrightarrow B^A.
\]
The exponential form is characteristic of self-modeling in a Cartesian closed setting: a state determines a function which can itself be evaluated on states. Our central hypothesis is point-surjective completeness: every observer in $B^A$ is represented by some state.

Under this hypothesis, diagonal evaluation forces every endomorphism of $B$ to have a fixed point. The representing state has additional structure: it represents a transformed diagonal observer and supplies a literal loop in the transition graph of the transformation. The same theorem exposes the cost of completeness. A fixed-point-free map on $B$ makes completeness impossible, and every set with two distinct elements has such a map. We obtain an exact existence classification rather than only an obstruction.

A complementary theme is observational recovery. Given $f:A\to B$, consider every composite $h\circ f:A\to X$ as $X$ and $h:B\to X$ vary. Evaluation at the identity probe recovers $f$, giving a concrete Yoneda faithfulness principle. Thus recursive closure and behavioral identity emerge from two elementary operations: diagonal evaluation and composition.

The terminology of consciousness is an interpretive bridge rather than an identification. We do not claim that a fixed point suffices for phenomenal experience, or that biological states represent arbitrary functions. Instead, the model makes the assumptions behind a familiar metaphor explicit and derives their exact consequences.

\section{Self-models and diagonal observation}

\begin{definition}[Self-model]
Let $A$ and $B$ be sets. A \emph{self-model} with state space $A$ and observation space $B$ is a function
\[
I:A\longrightarrow(A\longrightarrow B).
\]
For $a,x\in A$, the value $I(a)(x)$ is the observation of $x$ made by the observer represented at $a$.
\end{definition}

Distinct states may represent the same observer; no injectivity is assumed. No topology, order, algebra, or metric is required.

\begin{definition}[Completeness]
A self-model is \emph{complete} if $I$ is surjective: for every observer $p:A\to B$, there exists $a\in A$ such that $I(a)=p$.
\end{definition}

Completeness is unrestricted and extensional. It quantifies over every $B$-valued function on $A$, including functions defined adversarially from the model itself.

\begin{definition}[Diagonal observation]
The \emph{diagonal observation} is $d:A\to B$ defined by
\[
d(a)=I(a)(a).
\]
\end{definition}

The first occurrence of $a$ selects the represented observer and the second supplies the same state as its input.

\begin{definition}[Strange-loop witness]
For $g:B\to B$, a state $a\in A$ is a \emph{strange-loop witness} when
\[
I(a)(x)=g(d(x))\quad\text{for every }x\in A
\]
and
\[
g(d(a))=d(a).
\]
The first condition is representational and the second dynamical.
\end{definition}

\section{The diagonal fixed-point theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strange-Loop Fixed-Point Theorem]
Let $I:A\to(A\to B)$ be complete. For every $g:B\to B$, there exists a strange-loop witness $a\in A$. Thus
\[
I(a)=\bigl(x\mapsto g(d(x))\bigr)
\quad\text{and}\quad
g(d(a))=d(a).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $p:A\to B$ by $p(x)=g(d(x))$. Completeness supplies $a$ with $I(a)=p$. Evaluating at $a$ gives
\[
d(a)=I(a)(a)=p(a)=g(d(a)).
\]
The chosen $a$ also satisfies the required representational identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Universal fixed-point property]
If a complete self-model with observation space $B$ exists, then every endomorphism $g:B\to B$ has a fixed point.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Use the theorem and take $b=d(a)$.
\end{proof}

No convergence argument is involved. There is no metric, continuity, compactness, or order. The fixed point is forced solely by representability and self-application. Moreover, the witness is not an arbitrary fixed value: its state represents the global observer obtained by transforming every diagonal observation.

\section{Orbit graphs and closed loops}

\begin{definition}[Orbit graph]
For $g:B\to B$, the orbit graph has vertex set $B$ and an edge from $x$ to $y$ exactly when $y=g(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Closed orbit]
For $b\in B$ and $n\in\mathbb N$, a closed orbit of length $n$ based at $b$ means
\[
g^n(b)=b,
\]
where $g^0=\id_B$ and $g^{n+1}=g\circ g^n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[A strange-loop witness is a graph self-loop]
If $a$ is a strange-loop witness for $g$, then $d(a)$ has an edge to itself in the orbit graph.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The witness equation $g(d(a))=d(a)$ is exactly the edge condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed points close at every length]
If $g(b)=b$, then $g^n(b)=b$ for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$. The case $n=0$ is immediate. If $g^n(b)=b$, then
\[
g^{n+1}(b)=g(g^n(b))=g(b)=b.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed orbits of all lengths]
If $I$ is complete, then for every $g:B\to B$ there exists $a\in A$ such that
\[
g^n(d(a))=d(a)
\]
for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the diagonal theorem, then the preceding lemma to $d(a)$.
\end{proof}

These are degenerate closed walks at a single vertex, not assertions of distinct cycles of every length. They nevertheless give a literal graph-theoretic realization of recursive closure.

\section{Fixed-point-free obstructions}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-Point-Free Obstruction]
If $g:B\to B$ satisfies $g(b)\ne b$ for every $b\in B$, then no self-model $I:A\to(A\to B)$ is complete.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Completeness would yield a fixed point by the universal fixed-point property, contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Boolean incompleteness]
For every state space $A$, no Boolean-valued self-model
\[
I:A\longrightarrow(A\longrightarrow\{0,1\})
\]
is complete.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Negation exchanges $0$ and $1$ and therefore has no fixed point. Apply the obstruction theorem.
\end{proof}

This proof works uniformly for finite and infinite state spaces. It has the shape of a liar-style diagonal: the missing observer returns the opposite of each state's self-observation.

\begin{theorem}[Observational collapse]
If $I:A\to(A\to B)$ is complete, then $B$ is a subsingleton: for all $x,y\in B$, one has $x=y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $x\ne y$. Define
\[
g(z)=
\begin{cases}
y,&z=x,\\ x,&z\ne x.
\end{cases}
\]
At $x$, the output is $y\ne x$; away from $x$, the output is $x$, which differs from the input. Thus $g$ has no fixed point, contradicting completeness by the obstruction theorem.
\end{proof}

Hence the universal fixed-point property is extremely restrictive for sets: it permits at most one observation.

\section{Exact classification}

\begin{lemma}[Inhabited state space]
If a complete self-model exists, then $A$ is nonempty.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Even if $A$ were empty, there would be a unique function $A\to B$. Surjectivity would require a state representing it, contradicting emptiness.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Inhabited observation space]
If a complete self-model exists, then $B$ is nonempty.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Choose $a\in A$ by the previous lemma. Then $d(a)=I(a)(a)$ belongs to $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of complete self-models]
For sets $A$ and $B$, a complete self-model $I:A\to(A\to B)$ exists if and only if:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $A$ is nonempty;
\item $B$ is nonempty;
\item $B$ is a subsingleton.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Necessity follows from the two inhabitation lemmas and observational collapse. Conversely, choose $a_0\in A$ and $b_0\in B$, and define $I(a)(x)=b_0$ for all $a,x\in A$. If $p:A\to B$ is arbitrary, subsingletonness gives $p(x)=b_0$ for every $x$. Hence $p$ is the constant observer represented at $a_0$, so $I$ is surjective.
\end{proof}

This classification is stronger than a cardinality estimate. Unrestricted completeness is not merely unlikely in a large system: it is impossible as soon as observations express one genuine distinction.

\section{Yoneda recovery and behavioral identity}

\begin{definition}[Precomposition action]
Given $f:A\to B$, a target set $X$, and a probe $h:B\to X$, define
\[
P_f(X,h)=h\circ f:A\to X.
\]
Thus $f$ sends maps out of $B$ to maps out of $A$ by precomposition.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Recovery at the identity]
For every $f:A\to B$,
\[
P_f(B,\id_B)=f.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The identity law gives $\id_B\circ f=f$ pointwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Yoneda faithfulness for sets]
Let $f,g:A\to B$. If, for every set $X$ and every $h:B\to X$,
\[
h\circ f=h\circ g,
\]
then $f=g$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $X=B$ and $h=\id_B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recovery of represented observers]
For every state $a\in A$, the represented observer $I(a)$ is recovered by applying its precomposition action to the identity probe on $B$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply recovery at the identity to $f=I(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Extensional identity]
If $a_1,a_2\in A$ satisfy
\[
h\circ I(a_1)=h\circ I(a_2)
\]
for every set $X$ and every $h:B\to X$, then $I(a_1)=I(a_2)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Yoneda faithfulness to the two represented observers.
\end{proof}

The conclusion concerns observers, not states. Without injectivity of $I$, two distinct internal states may realize the same extensional behavior.

\section{Finite algorithms and diagnostics}

For finite $A$ and $B$, represent $I$ as a square table: row $a$ contains the observer $I(a)$, and column $x$ records its value at $x$. The diagonal can be computed in $O(|A|)$ time. Given $g$, form the target row $p(x)=g(d(x))$ and compare it with every represented row. A direct search takes $O(|A|^2)$ equality tests. If the model is complete, a row must match; if no row matches, the target itself certifies incompleteness.

An exhaustive completeness test enumerates all $|B|^{|A|}$ observers and compares them with the represented rows. This requires $O(|A||B|^{|A|})$ time and potentially exponential space. The classification theorem gives a more efficient structural decision procedure for existence: if $B$ has at least two elements, reject; if $A$ and $B$ are nonempty and $B$ has at most one element, accept.

For orbit diagnostics, test whether $g(b)=b$. If true, all finite iterates close by the fixed-point lemma, so repeated simulation is unnecessary for certification. For arbitrary finite maps, a visited table finds eventual cycles in $O(|B|)$ time and space, while tortoise-and-hare detection uses constant auxiliary space.

A simple finite counting bound also illustrates incompleteness. A map from an $m$-element state space represents at most $m$ observers, whereas there are $q^m$ observers when $|B|=q$. Thus at least $\max(0,q^m-m)$ observers are omitted. This bound is independent of the stronger diagonal obstruction and may be useful when quantifying finite deficits.

\section{Interpretation, applications, and limits}

The diagonal captures one abstract feature of self-modeling: a state carries a perspective on the same kind of states, so the perspective can be applied to its own carrier. If the represented family is closed under the transformed diagonal construction, recursive stability follows.

Boolean negation reproduces the form of semantic paradox without introducing inconsistency. A complete Boolean self-model would need to represent the observer that negates each state's self-observation. At its representing state, the output would equal its own negation. The conclusion is not contradiction within the observation space but failure of the assumed completeness: the adversarial observer is omitted.

The classification therefore advises against reading completeness as ordinary cognitive richness. It quantifies over all set-theoretic functions, including maximally adversarial ones. Realistic models may restrict observers by computability, continuity, bounded complexity, causal accessibility, architecture, or available resources. In such settings the transformed diagonal observer may not be admissible, and nontrivial observation spaces can survive.

The Yoneda principle provides an operational notion of observer identity. If every downstream probe gives the same composite behavior, the represented observers are equal. Internal carriers can still differ, suggesting a mathematical distinction between functional perspective and physical realization.

The graph interpretation identifies the diagonal witness as a period-one orbit, but it does not establish attraction, robustness, or perturbative stability. Those notions require additional metric, topological, probabilistic, or order structure. Likewise, the loops here are repeated fixed vertices; richer strange-loop topology would require a directed realization and path-space invariants.

Most importantly, the term consciousness remains interpretive. The results do not show that fixed points are sufficient for experience. They show that a precise architecture of representation and self-application forces stable self-reference, while an unrestricted completeness ideal is incompatible with meaningful observational alternatives.

\section{Future directions}

Several extensions are natural:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Internalize the argument in a general Cartesian closed category using a point-surjective morphism $A\to B^A$.
\item Compare diagonal fixed points with least and greatest fixed points of monotone operators on complete lattices.
\item Replace orbit graphs by directed topological realizations and study cycles in their path spaces.
\item Express the recovery theorem through categorical representables and prove invariance of witnesses under isomorphisms of self-models.
\item Introduce guarded self-reference through a delay modality, potentially retaining unique guarded fixed points in nontrivial models.
\item Quantify finite incompleteness by bounding the number and structure of omitted observers.
\item Equip $B$ with a metric and relate approximate representability to approximate fixed points.
\item Analyze fibers of $I$ to distinguish identity of observers from identity of their state realizations.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A complete self-model assigns states to all possible observers. Diagonal evaluation turns transformed self-observation into a represented observer and forces a fixed point. The witness is a genuine self-loop in the orbit graph and remains closed under every finite number of iterations.

The same construction imposes an exact limit. Fixed-point-free transformations obstruct completeness, Boolean negation gives the simplest example, and complete self-models exist exactly when the state space is inhabited and the observation space is inhabited but subsingleton. Unrestricted extensional self-representation therefore becomes possible only after observational distinctions collapse.

The Yoneda viewpoint adds that represented observers are determined by all downstream actions and recovered at the identity probe. Together, diagonalization and probing articulate recursive closure and behavioral identity. They provide a precise bridge for reasoning about strange loops while showing why meaningful self-models must be partial, guarded, stratified, approximate, or otherwise restricted.

