<details><summary>Reveal the behavioral identity theorem</summary>

If $h\circ f=h\circ g$ for every target $X$ and every probe $h:B\to X$, choose $X=B$ and $h=\operatorname{id}_B$. Then $f=g$. For self-models, this identifies represented observers, though not necessarily the states that realize them.

</details>

## 5. What the bridge does—and does not—claim

The mathematics explains how expressive self-reference forces stable loops and why unrestricted completeness collapses nontrivial observation. It does not claim that a fixed point alone constitutes conscious experience. For broader context, compare [fixed-point theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fixed-point_theorem) and [Cartesian closed categories](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category).

